1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''NAPOLEON II KING OF ROME & *EMPEROR OF FRANCE & CO-PRINCE OF ANDORRA & DUKE OF REICHSTADT''' <br/>
2 CONT ['''*'''''Emperor of France'' being a long disputed title as he only held it for about a month, but with that Emperorship he held the undoubted title of Co-Prince of Andorra, a title fully recognized in the Principality of Andorra
2 CONC  which is co-ruled by the Bishop of Urgell and the ruler of France as seen here: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_co-princes_of_Andorra'']
2 CONT
2 CONT ''http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon_II_Bonaparte
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Nascimento: no Palácio das Tulherias. Morte: no Palácio de Schönbrunn.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Napoleon II, Emperor of the French, King of Rome, Duke of Reichstadt.  Reign 22 June-7 July 1815  Coronation 22 June 1815  Father Napoleon I of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother Marie Louise, Duchess of Parma
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 20 March 1811
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris, France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 22 July 1832 (aged 21)
2 CONT
2 CONT Schönbrunn Palace, Vienna, Austria 
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte, Duke of Reichstadt (March 20, 1811 – July 22, 1832) was the son of Napoleon Bonaparte and his second wife, Marie Louise of Austria. Known from birth as the King of Rome, he was styled as
2 CONC  His Majesty the King of Rome, which Napoleon I declared was the courtesy title of the heir-apparent. He was briefly the second Emperor of the French.
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoleon I declared him his heir-apparent. Three years later, the First French Empire — to which he was heir — collapsed, and Napoleon abdicated the throne in favour of his infant son, who was taken by the empress to Château de Bl
2 CONC ois in April 1814. In 1815, after his defeat at Waterloo, Napoleon again abdicated in favour of his son whom he had not seen since his exile to Elba.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chamber of Representatives and Chamber of Peers recognized him as Emperor from the moment of his father's abdication (June 22, 1815), but the entrance of the Allies into Paris on July 7 brought a rapid end to his fictive rule.
2 CONC  Napoleon II, aged 4, was residing in Austria with his mother and was probably never aware at the time that he had been proclaimed Emperor. The next Bonaparte to come to the throne of France (in 1851) took the name Napoleon III in
2 CONC  deference to his cousin's theoretical reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1815, the young prince, now known as "Franz," after his maternal grandfather, rather than as "Napoleon," was a virtual captive in Austria. He was awarded the title of Duke of Reichstadt in 1818. He died of tuberculosis at Sc
2 CONC hönbrunn Palace in Vienna on July 22, 1832. Upon the death of his stepfather, Neipperg, and the revelation that his mother had borne two illegitimate children to him prior to their marriage, Franz said to his friend, Prokesch von 
2 CONC Osten, "If Josephine had been my mother, my father would not have been buried at Saint Helena, and I should not be at Vienna. My mother is kind but weak; she was not the wife my father deserved". It has been suggested that his dea
2 CONC th was the result of deliberate lead or arsenic poisoning at the hands of agents of Metternich's police state.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was very close to Princess Sophie of Bavaria and it has been further suggested that he was the father to her son, the future ill-fated Emperor Maximilian I of Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1940, the remains of Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte were transferred from Vienna to the dome of Les Invalides in Paris. This was done as a gift to France from German dictator Adolf Hitler. The remains of Napoleon I 
2 CONC were moved there in 1840. For some time, the young prince rested beside his father. Later the remains of Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte were moved to the lower church. While most of his remains were transferred to Pari
2 CONC s, his heart and intestines remained in Vienna. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoleon II of France
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte, Duke of Reichstadt (March 20, 1811 – July 22, 1832) was the son of Napoleon Bonaparte and his second wife, Marie Louise of Austria. Known from birth as the King of Rome, he was styled as
2 CONC  His Majesty the King of Rome, which Napoleon I declared was the courtesy title of the heir-apparent. He was briefly the second Emperor of the French.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three years after his birth in Paris, the First French Empire — to which he was heir — collapsed, and Napoleon abdicated the throne in favour of his infant son, who was taken by the empress to Château de Blois in April 1814. In 18
2 CONC 15, after his defeat at Waterloo, Napoleon again abdicated in favour of his son whom he had not seen since his exile to Elba.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chamber of Representatives and Chamber of Peers recognized him as Emperor from the moment of his father's abdication (June 22, 1815), but the entrance of the Allies into Paris on July 7 brought a rapid end to his regime. The n
2 CONC ext Bonaparte to come to the throne of France (in 1851) took the name Napoleon III in deference to his cousin's mostly theoretical reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1815, the young prince, now known as "Franz," after his maternal grandfather, rather than as "Napoleon," was a virtual captive in Austria. He was awarded the title of Duke of Reichstadt in 1818. He died of tuberculosis at Sc
2 CONC hönbrunn Palace in Vienna on July 22, 1832. Upon the death of his stepfather, Neipperg, and the revelation that his mother had borne two illegitimate children to him prior to their marriage, Franz said to his friend, Prokesch von 
2 CONC Osten, "If Josephine had been my mother, my father would not have been buried at Saint Helena, and I should not be at Vienna. My mother is kind but weak; she was not the wife my father deserved".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been suggested[2] that his death was the result of deliberate lead or arsenic poisoning at the hands of agents of Metternich's police state.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was very close to Princess Sophie of Bavaria and it has been further suggested that he was the father to her son, the future ill-fated Emperor Maximilian I of Mexico.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1940, the remains of Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte were transferred from Vienna to the dome of Les Invalides in Paris. This was done as a gift to France from German dictator Adolf Hitler. The remains of Napoleon I 
2 CONC were moved there in 1840. For some time, the young prince rested beside his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later the remains of Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte were moved to the lower church. While most of his remains were transferred to Paris, his heart and intestines remained in Vienna. They are in Urn 42 in the "Heart Cry
2 CONC pt" (Herzgruft) and his viscera are in Urn 76 of the Ducal Crypt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte was also known as "The Eaglet" (L'Aiglon). Edmond Rostand wrote a play, L'Aiglon, about his life. Serbian composer Petar Stojanović composed an operetta "Napoleon II: Herzog von Reichstad
2 CONC t", which premiered in Vienna in the 1920s.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Publications
2 CONT
2 CONT Welschinger, Le roi de Rome, 1811-32, (Paris, 1897)
2 CONT
2 CONT Wertheimer, The Duke of Reichstadt, (London, 1905)
2 CONT
