1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Louis Marie Michel Joachim Napoleon Murat, Prince Murat was born on September 8, 1896 in Rocquencourt, Yvelines, France. He was the seventh of the eight children of Joachim Napoléon Murat, 5th Prince Murat and Mari
2 CONC e Cécile Ney d’Elchingen. The House of Murat, collectively known as Princes of Murat, is a noble family created by Napoleon I, Emperor of the French for his brother-in-law Joachim-Napoléon Murat, Marshal of France, Admiral of Fran
2 CONC ce, and King of the Two Sicilies. Joachim married Napoleon’s youngest sister Caroline.  Prince Louis Murat was great-great grandnephew of Napoleon I, Emperor of the French, and great great grandson of Napoleon’s sister Caroline Bo
2 CONC naparte and her husband Joachim Murat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Louis’ father, Joachim Napoléon Murat, 5th Prince Murat, was a childhood friend of Louis Napoléon, Prince Imperial, the only child of Napoléon III, Emperor of the French and his wife Eugénie de Montijo. Following the Franco
2 CONC -Prussian War of 1870, which resulted in the fall of Napoléon III, Joachim and his parents left France to accompany the Imperial Family into exile in England. Once peace returned in France, the Murats returned to France, where the
2 CONC y acted as an intermediary between the former emperor and his son and Bonapartist movement in France, which hoped to restore the House of Bonaparte and its style of government. After the deaths of the former emperor and his son, J
2 CONC oachim continued working with the Bonapartist movement.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1915, Louis volunteered with the 5th Cuirassier Regiment of the French Army. On August 17, 1916, the regiment was sent to the front, specifically to the village of Lihons, France on the plateau of Santerre, to the east of the R
2 CONC iver Somme where the Battle of the Somme, one of the deadliest battles in history, was being fought. Louis was killed in action by the explosion of a rifle grenade on August 21, 1916, north of Lihons, at the age of 19.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Murat family decided to bury Louis where he had died. His tomb is located outside the village of Lihons, in a wooded park which the Murat family gave to the village in 1961. Unfortunately in June of 2007, Louis’ tomb was vanda
2 CONC lized and the medallion under the eagle was stolen. To avoid further damage, the village of Lihons decided to keep the eagle in the city hall and then replaced the eagle and the medallion with plaster copies.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.unofficialroyalty.com/august-1916-royalty-and-world-war-i/
