1 NAME Jean Christophe Louis Ferdinand Albéric Napoléon /Napoléon/
2 GIVN Jean Christophe Louis Ferdinand Albéric Napoléon
2 _MARNM Napoléon
1 NAME Jean Christophe, prince Napoléon
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 JUL 1986
2 ADDR
3 CITY Saint-Raphaël
3 STAE Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur
3 CTRY France
1 OCCU Prince Napoleon
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Christophe_Napol%C3%A9on
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00161653&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Christophe, Prince Napoléon (Jean Christophe Louis Ferdinand Albéric Napoléon; born 11 July 1986) is, in the views of some monarchists, Head of the former Imperial House of France and heir of the legacy of Napoleon Bonaparte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family background
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Jean-Christophe was born in Saint-Raphaël, Var, France. He is the son of Prince Charles Napoléon and his first wife Princess Béatrice of Bourbon-Two Sicilies, daughter of the late Prince Ferdinand of Bourbon, Duke of Castro
2 CONC , a claimant to Headship of the former Royal House of the Two Sicilies. His parents divorced on 2 May 1989, a couple of months before Jean-Christophe's 3rd birthday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Christophe is the great-great-great-grandnephew of Emperor Napoleon I of France (who has no legitimate, direct descendants) through the emperor's younger brother, Jérôme, King of Westphalia. Through his mother he is a descend
2 CONC ant of King Louis XV of France and through his great-grandmother princess Clémentine of Belgium he is a descendant of Louis Philippe I, King of the French, who was the last King to rule France, although Emperor Napoleon III would 
2 CONC serve as its last monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Napoléon
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Christophe's grandfather, Louis, Prince Napoléon, died in 1997, stipulating in his will that he wished his 11-year-old grandson Jean-Christophe to succeed him as Head of the Imperial House of France rather than the boy's fath
2 CONC er, Charles, who had embraced republican principles and re-married without his father's authorization. Despite the dynastic dispute, Jean-Christophe's father has stated that "there will never be conflict" between him and his son o
2 CONC ver the imperial succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT Education and career
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Christophe studied at Lycée Saint Dominique, Neuilly from 2001 to 2004 gaining a Baccalauréat with Honours in the sciences and mathematics. From 2004 to 2006 he studied economics and mathematics at the Institut privé de prépa
2 CONC ration aux études supérieures (IPESUP) in Paris. Jean-Christophe joined the HEC School of Management in Paris in 2006 where he spent five years, graduating with a Master of Science in Management degree.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Christophe currently works in New York City as a Investment Banking Analyst for Morgan Stanley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Christophe is fluent in French, English and Spanish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles and styles
2 CONT
2 CONT  11 July 1986  – 3 May 1997: His Imperial Highness Prince Jean Christophe Napoléon
2 CONT
2 CONT  3 May 1997  – present: His Imperial Highness the Prince Napoléon
